  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure keeps northern New England dry through the end of\nthe week and the weekend, while an offshore ocean storm brings \nsome cloud cover into parts of the region this week.\n\nAn inversion around 925mb moves in aloft during the daytime \ntoday, keeping temps a bit cooler today. Additionally, high \ncloud cover spreads inland from the coastline as cirrus from the\noffshore storm moves northward.\n\nTemps rise a few more degrees ahead of a weak front on \nThursday, before falling back again on Friday. The ocean storm \nmakes its closest pass Wednesday night and Thursday, before \ntracking near the Canadian Maritimes. \n\nThe pressure gradient between the offshore storm and high \npressure building in from the northwest could cause gusty winds \nlater Thursday into Friday morning. Current forecast soundings \nindicate that northerly gusts could top 30 to 35 MPH.\n\nThe broad high pressure center then drifts southward from the \nend of the week through the weekend. Centered south of New \nEngland, the high brings a moderating trend through the weekend \nand early next week, along with continued dry conditions. Highs \nreturn to the 60s by Sunday, with some low 70s possible by \nMonday next week. A weak cold front is possible by Tuesday next \nweek, but at this point it looks mostly dry as well.",
